.TH AUDIOD 8 "2026-11-07" "audiod 3.1" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
audiod \- elogind\-driven per\-user audio session manager for Slackware
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /usr/sbin/audiod
.PP
.B /etc/rc.d/rc.audiod
.RI { start | stop | restart | status }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B audiod
is a per\-user audio session manager for non\-systemd Slackware. It gives the
PipeWire stack the ordered startup, readiness gating, per\-user lifecycle, and
clean teardown that
.B systemd \-\-user
provides elsewhere, built entirely from elogind, libslack
.BR daemon (1),
and shell. elogind is used unmodified.
.PP
It watches elogind's
.B org.freedesktop.login1
D\-Bus interface for
.BR UserNew ,
.BR UserRemoved ,
.B SessionNew
and
.BR SessionRemoved .
Because elogind reference\-counts a user's sessions,
.B UserNew
fires only on a user's first login and
.B UserRemoved
only on their last logout.
.PP
The session signals are not redundant. Where
.BR loginctl (1)
lingering is enabled for a user, elogind creates that user's object at boot,
before anybody has logged in \-\- so the later graphical login raises
.B SessionNew
and no
.B UserNew
at all. Both pairs are therefore handled; the handlers are idempotent, so
whichever signal arrives first takes effect and the other is a no\-op.
.B SessionRemoved
tears the stack down only once the user has no local seated session left.
.PP
On first login
.B audiod
starts
.BR pipewire ,
waits for its socket to appear, then starts
.B wireplumber
and
.BR pipewire\-pulse ,
each under a
.B daemon \-rB
supervisor. On last logout it tears the stack down, targeted by name. This
fixes the long\-standing race where the stock scripts start dependent daemons
before the PipeWire core is ready, causing delayed or muted audio.
.PP
.B audiod
runs as a foreground root reactor. On start it reconciles users already
logged in, then follows login1 signals via
.BR "gdbus monitor" ,
reconnecting if the stream drops. Services are launched as the target user via
.B setpriv
with a clean environment, so no second elogind session is opened.
.SH OPTIONAL HUB MODE
When
.B HUB_MODE=yes
in
.IR /etc/audiod/audiod.conf ,
.B audiod
also runs an optional hub extension (sourced from
.IR /usr/libexec/audiod/hub.sh )
that turns the machine into a party audio hub: Bluetooth phones play through
the box, playback can mirror to several outputs, and trusted LAN machines can
stream in. Hub features are restricted to members of the hub group (default
.BR audiohub ).
The first hub member to log in becomes the owner of the shared sound card and
Bluetooth adapter; a per\-owner watcher
.RI ( hub\-btwatch.sh )
stops Bluetooth cleanly across VT switches to prevent a speaker drone.
.PP
Later members are guests: audiod writes
.I ~/.config/wireplumber/wireplumber.conf.d/89\-audiod\-hub\-no\-bluez.conf
into their home, which turns WirePlumber's bluez monitor off, so a guest has
no Bluetooth at all while that file exists. It is removed when the guest logs
out, when the user later becomes owner, on the next login when hub mode no
longer applies to that user, and by
.BR audiod\-restore.sh .
.PP
.B HUB_OWNER
pins the owner to a user name. A name that is not an existing user is
ignored, logged as a WARNING, and the first\-login rule is used. A recorded
owner with no local seated session left is released when the next member
logs in.
.PP
With
.B HUB_MODE=no
(the default) the hub does nothing and behaviour is identical to the plain
session manager, except that a leftover guest drop\-in is still removed. See
.BR audioctl (1)
for the user\-facing hub commands.
.SH GATING
A login is served only if elogind reports it as seated
.RB ( Seat
non\-empty),
.BR Class=user ,
and
.BR Remote=no .
Remote and greeter sessions are ignored. If the system is set to PulseAudio,
or
.B AUDIO_SERVER=pulse
is configured,
.B audiod
stands down entirely.
.SH ENABLING
.B audiod
changes how PipeWire starts, so the stock autostart must be disabled first
(this does not switch to PulseAudio):
.PP
.RS
.EX
/usr/sbin/audiod\-takeover.sh
.EE
.RE
.PP
Then enable the service so it starts after elogind:
.PP
.RS
.EX
chmod +x /etc/rc.d/rc.audiod
# in /etc/rc.d/rc.local, after elogind:
[ \-x /etc/rc.d/rc.audiod ] && /etc/rc.d/rc.audiod start
.EE
.RE
.PP
To restore the stock start mechanism, run
.BR audiod\-restore.sh .
It also removes hub guest drop\-ins from users' homes.
.PP
.B removepkg audiod
removes everything through the package's uninstall script: rc.audiod,
.IR /etc/audiod ,
the rc.local start block, the takeover edits and the guest drop\-ins. Your own
extra files in
.I /etc/audiod
and the
.B audiohub
group are reported, not removed.
.B upgradepkg
does not trigger it.
Note that upgrading the
.B pipewire
package may re\-enable the stock
.IR profile.d/pipewire.sh ;
re\-run
.B audiod\-takeover.sh
if needed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/audiod
The reactor.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/audioctl
User control tool; see
.BR audioctl (1).
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/audiod/audiod\-lib.sh
Shared logic (gate, detection, stack ops).
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/audiod/hub.sh
Optional hub logic (sourced always; with HUB_MODE=no it only removes
leftover guest drop\-ins).
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/audiod/hub\-btwatch.sh
Per\-owner Bluetooth VT\-switch watcher.
.TP
.I /etc/audiod/audiod.conf
Configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/audiod/stack.conf
Ordered service list.
.TP
.I /etc/rc.d/rc.audiod
Init script (mode 0644 = off until enabled).
.TP
.I /run/audiod.pid
Reactor pidfile.
.TP
.I /run/audiod\-hub\-owner
Hub owner state (cleared on reboot).
.TP
.I /run/audiod\-hub\-owner.lock
Serialises ownership claims.
.TP
.I ~/.config/wireplumber/wireplumber.conf.d/89\-audiod\-hub\-no\-bluez.conf
Guest drop\-in: bluez monitor off for that user. Moved to
.I ~/.config/wireplumber/89\-audiod\-hub\-no\-bluez.conf.bak
when removed.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Key settings in
.IR /etc/audiod/audiod.conf :
.B AUDIO_SERVER
(auto/pipewire/pulse),
.B MANAGE_DBUS
(spawn a session bus for bus\-less logins),
.BR BUS_WAIT ,
.BR SEAT_WAIT ,
.BR PIPEWIRE_WAIT ,
.BR DEBUG .
Hub keys:
.BR HUB_MODE ,
.BR HUB_GROUP ,
.BR HUB_OWNER ,
.BR HUB_BT_AUTORESUME ,
.BR HUB_BT_AUTOPLAY ,
.BR COMBINE ,
.BR COMBINE_SLAVES ,
.BR COMBINE_WAIT ,
.BR HUB_PULSEWATCH_ENABLE ,
.BR HUB_PULSEWATCH_INTERVAL ,
.BR NET_TCP ,
.BR NET_ACL ,
.BR NET_PORT .
.SH REQUIREMENTS
elogind exposing the login1 interface; psmisc
.RB ( fuser );
libslack
.BR daemon (1);
PipeWire; and
.B pam_elogind
in the login PAM stack. Hub mode additionally needs
.B bluez
with
.B bluetoothd
running.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B audiod
logs to syslog with the tag
.BR audiod .
.PP
A hub that is missing while ordinary audio still works almost always means
.BR pactl (1)
is talking to a different server than
.BR wpctl (1):
a standalone PulseAudio daemon and
.B pipewire\-pulse
both want
.IR /run/user/$uid/pulse/native ,
and whichever binds it first receives every
.B pactl
call. Check with
.B "pactl info | grep \(aqServer Name\(aq"
and
.BR "pgrep \-a pulseaudio" .
.BR audiod\-doctor.sh (8)
repairs the two causes: an
.I /etc/xdg/autostart/pulseaudio.desktop
reinstated by a package upgrade, and a per\-user
.I ~/.config/pulse/client.conf
that shadows the system one (libpulse uses the per\-user file
.I instead
of the system file rather than merging them, so a system\-wide
.B "autospawn = no"
silently stops applying).
.SH LIMITATIONS
Two different local users cannot share one sound card at once (a kernel/ALSA
limit, identical under systemd); the inactive session falls back to Dummy
Output. Kernel\-level device enumeration delays are outside its control. For
cross\-user sharing over the network, see the separate
.B audioshare
project.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR audioctl (1),
.BR audiod\-doctor.sh (8),
